---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { playlists, architectures } from '../../data/power-bi';
const examples = (await getCollection('examples')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---
<BaseLayout title="Power BI" description="Power BI teaching, tutorials, model architectures and examples by Josh Levent.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Current</p>
    <h1>Power BI</h1>
    <p class="lead">Six years of teaching Power BI to people who are not data professionals, and the reference material that grew out of it.</p>

    <p>Since 2020 I have taught the <a href="https://www.bfh.ch/en/continuing-education/specialist-courses/powerbi-bootcamp/">Power BI Bootcamp</a> at the Bern University of Applied Sciences, a five-day short course that takes participants from a spreadsheet to a published, refreshing report. Most of them work in marketing, finance, HR or operations rather than IT, so the course is built around one question: what do you actually need to know to answer your own questions with your own data? Inside the university I am the go-to person for Power BI, where I have built reporting on a Microsoft Fabric data platform and run internal trainings and workshops.</p>

    <p>Everything on this page is published under the MIT licence. I have no affiliation with Microsoft.</p>

    <h2 id="tutorials">Video tutorials</h2>
    <p>Recordings I make for the course, published as playlists on YouTube.</p>
  </article>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="card-grid">
      {playlists.map((p) => (
        <a class="card" href={p.href}>
          <p class="eyebrow">YouTube playlist</p>
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.text}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2 id="architectures">Model architectures</h2>
    <p>Three reference architectures, from the one every beginner should master to a full Fabric lakehouse set-up, each with a diagram to download. <a href="/power-bi/architectures">Read the architectures →</a></p>
  </article>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="card-grid">
      {architectures.map((a) => (
        <a class="card" href={`/power-bi/architectures#${a.id}`}>
          <img class="card-image card-image--contain" src={a.image} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <h3>{a.title}</h3>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>
    <p>Worked solutions with DAX and Power Query code and a <code>.pbix</code> file to open in Power BI Desktop. Download the file and adapt it, or copy the code and rename the table and column references to match your data. The Power Query examples generate their own sample data, which you replace with your own.</p>
  </article>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="card-grid">
      {examples.map((e) => (
        <a class="card" href={`/power-bi/examples/${e.id}`}>
          <h3>{e.data.title}</h3>
          {e.data.description && <p>{e.data.description}</p>}
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Further help</h2>
    <p>For anything these pages do not cover, start with the official documentation: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/">Power BI</a>, the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/">DAX reference</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-query/">Power Query</a>. Microsoft's own guidance on <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/center-of-excellence-business-intelligence-solution-architecture">BI solution architecture</a> and the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/tutorial-lakehouse-introduction">lakehouse end-to-end tutorial</a> complement the architectures above. If you get stuck, the <a href="https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Power-BI-forums/ct-p/powerbi">Power BI community forums</a> are the place to ask.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
